===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|brytten}}, {{m|enm|brutten}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|brittian}}, {{m|ang|bryttian||to divide, dispense, distribute, rule over, possess, enjoy the use of}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*brutjaną||to break, divide}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰrewd-||to break}}. Cognate with {{cog|is|brytja||to chop up, break in pieces, slaughter}}, {{cog|sv|bryta||to break, fracture, cut off}}, {{cog|da|bryde||to break}} and Albanian {{m|sq|brydh||I make crumbly, friable, soft}}. Related to {{cog|ang|brytta||dispenser, giver, author, governor, prince}}, {{cog|ang|brēotan||to break in pieces, hew down, demolish, destroy, kill}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|britt}}
* {{l|en|brite}} {{qualifier|dialectal}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|britt}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[break]] in pieces; [[divide]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bruise]]; [[indent]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[fall]] out or [[shatter]] (as overripe hops or grain).
# {{lb|en|intransitive|dialectal}} To [[fade]] away; [[alter]].

